---
title: Server router
summary: The routing model of SvelteKit as a typed, programmatic table. Pages, layouts, loads, actions, endpoints in one file. App['/path'] gives each component its data, typed, with no codegen.
related:
  - app/spa-router
  - app/dashboards
  - regions/held-regions
---

`ogygia/router` is **the routing model of SvelteKit, expressed as values** instead of a file tree. One ordinary module declares the full route table: pages, layouts, loads, actions, endpoints. The types thread through it. Thus each component reads its `data`, `params`, and `form` fully typed, with **no codegen and no `$types` import**.

It runs on the **server**: it matches the request, runs the load cascade, and renders the page through its layout chain. It is the counterpart to the [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router), which navigates between those pages on the client. Use either alone. Together, the server router defines routes, and the SPA router moves between them.

All that a Kit user knows carries over: `+layout.svelte` loads, the data cascade, `page.data`, `+error.svelte`, form actions, `redirect`/`error`, `$app/state`, `$app/navigation`. The one difference: each concept is an **imported function**, not a magic filename.

::: tip When to use
Use it when your routes are *computed*: a CMS whose paths come from a database, a tool whose pages are generated, a package that ships its own routes. Or when you want the full route table, its loaders, and its components in one place, not scattered across `+page.svelte` / `+page.server.ts` / `+server.ts`. For a few ordinary pages, the file routes of Kit are simpler. This is not a replacement that you must adopt.
:::

## The whole thing in one file

```ts title=src/lib/router.ts
import { routes, page, layout, load, redirect, error, fail } from 'ogygia/router';
import AppShell from '$lib/views/AppShell.svelte';
import Home from '$lib/views/Home.svelte';
import Doc from '$lib/views/Doc.svelte';
import Login from '$lib/views/Login.svelte';
import ErrorPage from '$lib/views/ErrorPage.svelte';
import { docs, auth } from '$lib/server';

// A layout is a NAMED table→table wrapper: its component, its load, its error boundary.
const app = layout('app', AppShell, {
  load: async (c) => ({ user: await auth.user(c.cookies) }),
  error: ErrorPage,
});

export const router = routes(
  app({
    '/':            page(Home),
    '/docs/[slug]': page(Doc, {
      load: async (c) => {
        const doc = await docs.get(c.params.slug);   // c.params.slug: string, from the pattern
        if (!doc) error(404, 'No such doc');          // throws — renders the nearest error page
        return { doc: await doc.body, title: doc.data.title };
      },
    }),
    '/login': page(Login, {
      actions: {
        default: async (c) => {
          const form = await c.request.formData();
          if (!(await auth.login(form))) return fail(400, { incorrect: true });
          redirect(303, '/');
        },
      },
    }),
    '/api/[id]': { GET: (c) => c.json(docs.raw(c.params.id)) },   // an endpoint — the +server.ts shape
  }),
);

export type App = typeof router.$infer;   // the ONE type map components index
```

That is the full surface. The rest of this page is each piece in detail.

## Pages

A page is a Svelte component plus its `+page.server.ts`-shaped server object:

```ts
'/':            page(Home),                              // no server logic
'/docs/[slug]': page(Doc, { load: fn }),                 // a load
'/login':       page(Login, { actions: { default } }),   // form actions
'/edit/[id]':   page(Editor, { load, actions, params, search }),   // all of it
```

The second argument mirrors `+page.server.ts` exactly: `load`, `actions`, plus the input schemas `params` and `search` (see [Typed input](#typed-input)). It is always an object. Write a bare `page(Home)` when there is nothing to declare.

A routed page is **inert**: it renders as `csr = false` server HTML, with zero JavaScript. Interactivity comes from marked island imports *inside* the component (`with { wake }`), exactly as in all other parts of ogygia. See [Pages carry region dials](#pages-carry-region-dials) to wake or defer the page itself.

## Layouts

`layout(name, Component, { load?, error? })` returns a **table→table wrapper**. Apply it to a sub-table, and each page inside renders within that layout:

```ts
const app = layout('app', AppShell, { load: async (c) => ({ user: await user(c) }) });

routes(
  app({
    '/':       page(Home),
    '/about':  page(About),
  }),
);
```

The layout component is a Kit layout: `{ data, children }`, `{@render children()}`:

```svelte title=AppShell.svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import type { App } from '$lib/router';
  let { data, children }: App['(app)'] = $props();
</script>
<nav>signed in as {data.user.name}</nav>
{@render children()}
```

### Nesting

A nested layout is a wrapped sub-table spread into another. A page inside both wraps renders inside both, and its `data` merges both loads:

```ts
const app  = layout('app',  AppShell,  { load: loadUser });
const dash = layout('dash', DashShell, { load: loadTeam });

routes(
  app({
    '/':  page(Home),                                 // AppShell
    ...dash({
      '/dashboard': page(Dashboard),                  // AppShell › DashShell, data = { user, team }
    }),
  }),
);
```

### Breaking out of a layout

Kit resets a page to a named ancestor with `+page@name.svelte` (or `+page@.svelte` for the root). Here it is **placement**: you put the route in the table, wrapped by exactly the layouts that you want. More explicit: the reset point *is* the structure, not a filename annotation.

```ts
routes(
  app({
    ...dash({
      '/dashboard':          page(Dashboard),   // app › dash
    }),
    '/dashboard/settings':   page(Settings),    // app only     — reset out of `dash`  (Kit's @(app))
  }),
  '/login':                  page(Login),        // no chrome    — reset to root         (Kit's @)
);
```

| Kit | ogygia/router |
| --- | --- |
| `+layout-foo.svelte` (a named layout) | `layout('foo', Foo)` |
| `+page@foo.svelte` (reset to `foo`) | place the route inside `foo(…)` but outside the inner wraps |
| `+page@.svelte` (reset to root) | place the route outside all wraps |

## Loads and the data cascade

A load is a function of `c` that returns the data of the page. Declare it inline, pull it out with the `Load<'/pattern'>` type helper, or wrap it with `load()` when it must be **shared** across loads:

```ts
// inline
page(Doc, { load: async (c) => ({ doc: await docs.get(c.params.slug) }) }),

// pulled out, typed by the pattern
import type { Load } from 'ogygia/router';
const docLoad: Load<'/docs/[slug]'> = async (c) => ({ doc: await docs.get(c.params.slug) });
page(Doc, { load: docLoad }),
```

**The cascade is the one from Kit, verbatim.** The `data` of a page is the **merge** of each load on its branch: each layout, then the page, outermost first, last wins. A component reads the full merge:

```svelte title=Dashboard.svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import type { App } from '$lib/router';
  let { data }: App['/dashboard'] = $props();
  //   data.user   — from the app layout's load
  //   data.team   — from the dash layout's load
  //   data.stats  — from this page's load
</script>
```

`page.data` (through `$app/state`) is that same merged object. Thus a component in any location reads `page.data.user`: exactly where a Kit user looks for it.

### Sharing without waterfalls

Loads on one branch run **concurrently** by default (the behavior of Kit). When one load truly needs the value of another, wrap the shared one with `load()`, and `await` it. It runs **one time per request** (memoized). Thus there is no double fetch:

```ts
const session = load(async (c) => ({ user: await auth.user(c.cookies) }));

const app   = layout('app',   AppShell, { load: async (c) => await session(c) });
const admin = layout('admin', AdminShell, {
  load: async (c) => {
    const { user } = await session(c);       // shared with the app layout — runs ONCE
    if (!user.admin) redirect(303, '/login');
    return { user };
  },
});
```

This is the `await parent()` of Kit, generalized and typed. Sequencing exists exactly where a real data dependency exists, and in no other place. Waterfall *and* waste are both structurally impossible.

## Form actions

Actions are the form actions of Kit: a POST handler that reads `FormData` itself. `fail(status, data)` returns validation data to the typed `form` prop. `redirect()` throws on success:

```ts
page(Login, {
  actions: {
    default: async (c) => {
      const form = await c.request.formData();
      const user = await auth.login(form);
      if (!user) return fail(400, { email: form.get('email'), incorrect: true });
      redirect(303, '/dashboard');
    },
    logout: async (c) => { await auth.logout(c); redirect(303, '/'); },
  },
});
```

Named actions use the `?/name` URL convention of Kit (`default` is the unnamed one). The `form` prop is typed from the returns of the actions:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  let { data, form }: App['/login'] = $props();
</script>
{#if form?.incorrect}<p>Wrong email or password.</p>{/if}
```

Progressive enhancement rides the [form continuity](/docs/app/spa-router) of ogygia. No `use:enhance` import is needed on a `csr = false` page.

## Endpoints

An endpoint is the `+server.ts` shape, written as an object of **uppercase verb handlers**:

```ts
'/api/[id]': {
  GET:    (c) => c.json(read(c.params.id)),
  DELETE: (c) => { remove(c.params.id); return new Response(null, { status: 204 }); },
},
```

`c.params` is typed from the **table key**: a bare handler reads `c.params.id`, with no pattern restated. When you need typed params in a *pulled-out* handler, or a **body schema**, use the exported verb wrappers `GET` / `POST` / `PUT` / `DELETE` / `PATCH`: the same "bare or wrapper" choice as `load()`/`action()`:

```ts
import { POST, type Handler } from 'ogygia/router';

// body schema on POST/PUT/PATCH → validated JSON body into c.input (400 on bad input)
'/posts': { POST: POST(NewPostSchema, (c) => c.json(create(c.input))) },

// pulled out + typed by the pattern
const readPost: Handler<'/posts/[id]'> = (c) => c.json(read(c.params.id));
'/posts/[id]': { GET: readPost },
```

`GET`/`DELETE` take no body schema (their input is path params + query, already typed). `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` are handled automatically from the verb set. A wrong method 405s, with an `Allow` header.

## The context `c`

Each load, action, and handler receives `c`: the request surface of Kit:

| `c` member | what |
| --- | --- |
| `c.params` | path params, typed from the pattern (or the output of the `params` schema) |
| `c.search` | query params: `Record<string, string>`, or the typed output of the `search` schema |
| `c.input` | the validated JSON body of a `POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH` body schema |
| `c.url` / `c.request` / `c.route.id` | the request |
| `c.cookies` / `c.locals` / `c.platform` / `c.setHeaders` / `c.getClientAddress` | Kit `RequestEvent` pass-throughs (present when mounted in Kit) |
| `c.fetch` | the cookie-forwarding fetch of Kit; same-origin requests resolve in-process |
| `c.json()` / `c.redirect()` / `c.text()` | Response shortcuts |
| `c.href('/docs/[slug]', { slug })` | a rename-safe URL to a route in this table |
| `c.state` | a per-request scratch bag (a guard writes, a handler reads) |
| `c.visitor` | THE identity, resolved one time, lazily (below) |

### One identity: `c.visitor`

Declare who the request is **one time**, on the table, and read it in all locations:

```ts
routes(table, {
  visitor: (c) => session_from(c.cookies),   // → { sub: 'u123', roles: [...] } or undefined
});
```

`c.visitor` is lazy and memoized. The resolver runs at most one time per request, and only when something reads it. [Experiments](/docs/app/experiments) stick on it. [Mounts](/docs/app/federation) sign it onward to other teams. Loads personalize with it. When the request arrived through a signed federation hop, the signature-bound claims of the upstream **win** over the local resolver. Proof beats config.

## Control flow: redirect, error, fail

Thrown from a load, action, or handler. The exact names of Kit, imported from `ogygia/router`:

```ts
import { redirect, error, fail } from 'ogygia/router';

redirect(303, '/login');            // → a redirect Response
error(404, 'Not found');            // → the nearest error boundary (page) / JSON (endpoint)
return fail(400, { invalid: true }); // action only — re-render with `form` = data, given status
```

A thrown `error()` on a page branch renders the **nearest** layout `error` component, inside the chrome that survived (the `+error.svelte` semantics of Kit). Set the root boundary with `routes(table, { error: ErrorPage })`. Scope one to a subtree with `layout('name', C, { error })`. On an endpoint, `error()` becomes a JSON response.

## Typed input

Path params are strings. A [Standard Schema](https://standardschema.dev) (Valibot, Zod, ArkType, …) coerces and validates them. The coerced type flows into `App`. This is truly **beyond Kit**, and fully opt-in. With no schema, `params` is pattern-typed exactly as in Kit.

```ts
import * as v from 'valibot';

'/post/[id]': page(Post, {
  params: v.object({ id: v.pipe(v.string(), v.transform(Number), v.number()) }),  // "abc" → 404
  search: v.object({ page: v.optional(v.number(), 1) }),                          // ?page=x → 400
  load: async (c) => ({ post: await posts.get(c.params.id) }),   // c.params.id: number, c.search.page: number
}),
```

- **`params`**: coerce/validate path params. Garbage → **404** (it is a nonexistent resource). `App['/post/[id]']['params']` becomes the output of the schema.
- **`search`**: type the query. Bad input → **400**, or lenient with schema defaults. `App['/post/[id]']['search']` too.
- **endpoint body**: `POST(schema, fn)` validates the JSON body into `c.input`. A bad body → **400**. Like the typed argument of a `command` remote function.

## Typed everything: `$infer`

`typeof router.$infer` is a phantom-typed map. It exists only at the type level (a runtime read throws; always write `typeof router.$infer`). Export it one time. Components index it:

```ts
export type App = typeof router.$infer;
```

- `App['/docs/[slug]']` → `{ data, form, params, search }` for that page
- `App['(app)']` → `{ data, children }` for the `app` layout

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import type { App } from '$lib/router';
  let { data, form, params }: App['/docs/[slug]'] = $props();
</script>
```

It is computed from the **loads and schemas**, never from component types. Thus there is no cycle and no codegen. Layout keys use the group spelling of Kit (`(app)`). Path keys always start with `/`. Thus the two never collide. Duplicate layout names are a build error.

::: tip No `$types`, no cast
This replaces the generated `./$types` of Kit. The component declaration `App['/path']` *is* the check: props are defined as the type of the route. Change the return of a load, and the component squiggles: exactly the flow of Kit, with zero generated files.
:::

## Typed API client

The table already knows the path grammar, body schema, and payload of each endpoint. `api()` gives that to a consumer, as a typed fetch client:

```ts
export type App = typeof app.$infer;                 // the map the app already exports

import { api } from 'ogygia/router/client';           // browser-safe (islands can import it)
const svc = api<App>('https://svc.internal');

const post = await svc.get('/posts/[id]', { params: { id: '1' } });   // payload: typed
await svc.post('/posts', { body: { title } });                        // body: schema-typed
```

- **Plain returns are the payload.** `GET: (c) => ({ id: c.params.id })` serializes to JSON (`finalize()`). Thus its type flows to the caller. A handler that returns a raw `Response` types as `unknown`: its payload is sealed inside.
- Paths are constrained to keys that carry the verb (page paths never do), `params` is required exactly when the pattern has some, and a `POST(schema, fn)` body types from the schema.
- Non-2xx throws `ApiError` with the status and the parsed error body (a schema 400 carries its issues).
- Pass `{ headers }` for auth on each call, and `{ fetch }` for the cookie-forwarding fetch of a Kit event, or a test stub.

## Pages carry region dials

A routed page is only a component. Thus its **wake / render** schedule rides as an import attribute on the component that you pass to `page()`: the same dials as a marked island import:

```ts
import Chart from '$lib/views/Chart.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
import Greeting from '$lib/views/Greeting.svelte' with { render: 'deferred' };

'/dashboard': page(Chart),                              // wakes when scrolled into view
'/':          page(Greeting, { fallback: Skeleton }),   // deferred page + its loading slot
```

`render: 'deferred'` gives a **partially-prerendered page from the route table**: a static shell, with a per-visitor hole, and the `fallback` snippet as its loading UI. See [held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions) and [server islands](/docs/regions/server-islands).

A **dynamic** pattern prerenders when it declares its param sets: the per-route `entries` export of Kit, as a page option:

```ts
'/posts/[id]': page(Post, {
  entries: async () => (await posts.refs()).map((p) => ({ id: p.id })),
  load: (c) => ({ post: posts.get(c.params.id) }),
}),
```

Each set fills the pattern into the `entries()` crawl list of the router. A dynamic page without them is crawled, or SSR'd live, as before.

## Streamed pages: yield regions

"The next state of a slot is a region" already powers [live regions](/docs/regions/held-regions). The same vocabulary operates on the **initial response**. A page slot can be an async generator. The first yield renders, and the page **flushes immediately**. Each later yield rides down the *same* response, and swaps into place as it arrives. One connection, closed when the generator ends.

```ts
'/report': page(async function* (c, data) {
  yield '<div class="skeleton">crunching…</div>';        // flushes NOW
  yield region(Report, { data: await slow_numbers(c) }); // swaps in when ready
}),
```

- Yield regions, or raw html strings. A component region bakes with its islands inside. Islands are self-waking custom elements. Thus they connect and hydrate the moment that the late chunk lands. No orchestration layer.
- Several late yields apply in order. Progressive states are free.
- A throw after the flush becomes an inline error card in the slot. The page always closes cleanly.
- The honest trades: status, title, and headers go out with the first yield. A late failure cannot change an already-sent 200, and `setHeaders` after the flush is lost. Non-GET requests buffer to the final yield (actions need a full answer).
- Streamed responses carry `Cache-Control: no-transform`. A compressing intermediary buffers while it gzips, which would collapse the early flush into one late paint. When your CDN compresses regardless, exempt these routes (or accept buffered delivery there).
- A component that you yield from a server-only module needs a mark (`with { region: 'raw' }`, or a wake). A plain import has no client leg. Thus its region cannot bake. A mistake here fails loudly, into the error card of the slot.

**Streamed load data** needs no generator at all. Give a *promise of a region* from the load to a `<Region of={…}>`, and the region resolves down the initial response: a placeholder in the flushed document, the baked region as a completion-order chunk, and the islands inside wake on adoption. Put as many such holes on one page as you want. Each fails into its own error card.

```ts
'/report': page(Report, {
  load: () => ({ pending: (async () => region(Numbers, await slow()))() }),
}),
```

```svelte
<Region of={data.pending}>
  {#snippet placeholder()}<div class="skeleton" />{/snippet}
</Region>
```

(This rides the request scope of the ogygia handle: standard wiring. A rejection becomes the error card of that slot, and never touches its neighbors.)

## Flag-gated routes: `when()`

Wrap a table entry (a page, an endpoint object, a [`mount()`](/docs/app/federation)), and it exists only when the gate says so:

```ts
import { when } from 'ogygia/router';
import { checkoutV2, exportsFlag, cmsRollout } from '$lib/flags';

routes({
  '/checkout-v2': when(checkoutV2, page(NewCheckout, { load })),  // staged rollout of a whole page
  '/api/export':  when(exportsFlag, { GET: GET(export_csv) }),    // endpoint kill switch
  '/cms/[...rest]': when(cmsRollout, mount(cms)),                 // per-cohort fragment rollout
});
```

**Off means that the route does not exist** for that request. Under an owned `base`, the 404/error page of the app answers. Without one, the router falls through to the rest of the app. Thus a gated-off ogygia route can be served by a plain Kit route at the same path, during a migration.

The gate is any `(c) => boolean`, which is exactly what a boolean [`flag()`](/docs/app/experiments) is. Thus a [vendor-decided](/docs/app/experiments#openfeature-decide-source) kill switch gates full routes (the gate runs after the source primes). The decision is sticky per visitor. And it [carries across federation](/docs/app/federation), like each flag read. `$infer` sees the entry exactly as if unwrapped.

## Mounting

Export the router. Serve it at either seam.

```ts title=src/hooks.server.ts
import { sequence } from '@sveltejs/kit/hooks';
import { handle as ogygiaHandle } from 'ogygia/server';
import { router } from '$lib/router';

export const handle = sequence(ogygiaHandle(), router.handle);
```

`router.handle` dispatches a match, or falls through to the rest of the app (a good citizen for URLs that it does not own). Or own a subtree from a Kit catch-all:

```ts title="src/routes/[...path]/+server.ts"
import { router } from '$lib/router';
export const GET = (event) => router.fetch(event.request, event);
export const entries = router.entries;      // static routes + declared dynamic entries → Kit's crawl
export const prerender = 'auto';
```

`router.fetch(request, event?)` returns the `Response`, or `null` on no-match. Table options:

```ts
routes(table, {
  base: '/app',                 // mount prefix, stripped before matching
  slash: 'never',               // trailing-slash policy — 'ignore' | 'never' | 'always' (308s)
  error: ErrorPage,             // root error boundary
  miss: (c) => c.error(404),    // response for an unmatched path under `base`
  load: authGuard,              // a table-wide load before EVERY route — a guard with no chrome
  visitor: session,             // THE identity → c.visitor (see above)
  experiments: [csrExp],        // auto-carried into federation hops — see Experiments
});
```

The table-wide `load` is the root `+layout.server.ts` load of Kit, without a component. It runs before each route (pages *and* endpoints). It throws `redirect`/`error` to gate. Its data merges into each page. It is the place for auth, when there is no shared shell to hang it on.

## Kit ↔ ogygia/router

| SvelteKit | ogygia/router |
| --- | --- |
| `src/routes/` tree | one `routes({ … })` table |
| `+page.svelte` | first arg of `page()` |
| `+page.server.ts` (`load`, `actions`) | second arg of `page()` |
| `+layout.svelte` + its load | `layout(name, Component, { load })` |
| `+error.svelte` | `{ error: Component }` on `layout()` / `routes()` |
| `+layout@` / `+page@` reset | placement (put the route outside the wrap) |
| `(group)` folder | the layout name in `App['(name)']` |
| `+server.ts` | an `{ GET, POST, … }` object |
| `await parent()` | read the merged `data`; in a load, `await sharedLoad(c)` |
| `page.data` | the leaf page's merged `data` |
| `depends` + `invalidate` / `invalidateAll` | `invalidateAll()` (re-runs the branch's loads) |
| `throw redirect()` / `error()` | same names, from `ogygia/router` |
| `./$types` (`PageData`, …) | `App['/path']` — no codegen |
| `$app/state`, `$app/navigation` | work unchanged on router pages |

::: warning Two routers, one app
This page is the **server** router: the route table. The [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router) is separate: it intercepts link clicks, and navigates on the client. They compose, and each operates without the other.
:::
